  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1109 AM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nWe have been watching widespread rain and snow this morning\nquickly diminishing, with pockets of light precipitation/showers\ncontinuing. By this evening, we are expecting just a few showers\nlingering along the Montana border. We are looking for dry\nconditions with temperatures briefly warming up Sunday, bouncing\nback about 5 degrees or so. \n\nThe next low begins to slowly push inland tomorrow night and\nMonday. Progression continues to slow, keeping the moisture\npretty much over the central mountains through the afternoon.\nThis will knock down temperature a couple of degrees over the\ncentral mountains and south-central Idaho, while little to no\nchange forecast farther east. The storm finally swings across\nthe state thorugh Tuesday night, but again we are still seeing a\ntrend toward splitting like today's storm. That is creating\nuncertainty and low confidence in more specific and definitive \ntotals for rain and snow. The latest forecast from the Blend of\nModels has general up 0.20\" across the Snake Plain, Magic\nValley, and low to mid elevation mountain valleys. For favored\nupslope areas (Wood River Valley and surrounding mountains, and\nalong the Montana border) amounts are pushing 0.50\". The \npattern is somewhat similar across the South Hills/Albion \nMountains through the Bear River Range. For other mountain \nranges, we are pushing 0.25-0.35\" at higher elevations. The\nbigger difference right now is snow levels. They should remain\nat 6500-8000ft before the storm impacts all of eastern Idaho on\nTuesday. It will be a race between the colder air arriving and\nprecipitation ending on the back side of the storm to see snow\nat lower elevations. Right now, snow levels remain between\n5000-6500ft during that transition. The lower end of range for\nsnow totals actually drops them low enough to see at least snow\nfalling at lower elevations, but not necessarily sticking. That\nsaid, we are expecting some accumulating snow down to around\n6000ft...but impactful snow amounts remaining above 7000ft. Just\nlike with this storm, some of the ensembles and AI versions of\nthese models are producing almost double the precipitation\nnumbers than the Blend and WPC are officially forecasting.\nLooking back at this storm, early amounts actually ended up\n25-50% higher than what the Blend and WPC was\nforecasting...especially in upslope areas and along the cold\nfront. Something to watch as we quickly approach this round.\nTemperatures continue to trend colder mid to late next week. \nWith the models all over the place with how the pattern may \nunfold, let's just say IF we do get some decent precipitation to\nfall with colder temperatures...snow at valley floors is \ncertainly possible.",
